{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XwX8gAbtK6LKg4c6pJyvnm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HX8FQJAbFGfFhv56QeKqsu","name":"Glob","input":{"pattern":"**/*.json","path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/_project/data"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10129,"cache_read_input_tokens":46653,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10129,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vy8zLpv18F3s9FgtDiisiH","session_id":"891f265c-8c20-445c-964a-94d66c0b1d82","uuid":"d64f707f-29b7-4ff5-9f6c-f21128bde8cd"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011paJaScWXDQCtRJ6d26kLR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01P86NUJis8A2ACvuPa9SU6B","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 2 (R2)** in a blind peer review for an AAAI submission. Your focus area is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## AAAI Scoring Scale (1-10)\n- 10: Award-quality\n- 9: Very strong accept\n- 8: Strong accept\n- 7: Accept\n- 6: Weak accept\n- 5: Borderline\n- 4: Weak reject\n- 3: Reject\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"The Hidden Second Factor: Model×Item Interaction Accounts for 34% of LLM Evaluation Variance\"\n\n**Core idea:** Applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) from educational measurement to decompose LLM evaluation variance across multiple facets simultaneously. Extends Mesting (2026) — who applied G-theory to LLM eval with 5 facets on a single benchmark finding judge identity as dominant (43.8%) — by (a) adding infrastructure as facet, (b) treating model as crossed facet to expose model×item interaction, (c) extending to 4 benchmarks, (d) fixing judge to exact-match to isolate infrastructure×methodology.\n\n**Three claimed contributions:**\n1. **Model×item interaction quantification**: model×item=33.6% of cross-model MMLU variance [95% CI: 28.4–37.9%], the \"hidden second factor.\" Single-model analyses inflate item dominance from 42%→61% by conflating universal and model-specific difficulty. D-study from 3→4 models validates projective machinery (Δ=0.004).\n2. **IRT vs G-theory direct comparison**: First head-to-head. G-theory requires 1.6–4.1× more items than IRT on same benchmarks. Ratio is benchmark-dependent: MMLU 4.1× (items have wide discrimination range, IRT efficient) vs HellaSwag 1.6× (uniform discrimination, IRT less efficient). Explains why field hasn't converged on \"is IRT subsampling sufficient?\"\n3. **Approximate benchmark-invariance of variance structure**: Across MMLU, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, item dominance ranges only 5.7pp (38.4%–44.1%). Originally hypothesized a gradient from knowledge→reasoning benchmarks; gradient died (R²=0.12). Reframed as positive: practitioners can cautiously extrapolate MMLU-derived budgets with 3–6pp safety margin.\n\n**Related Work (as presented in paper, Section 2):**\n- *Variance decomposition in ML*: Bouthillier et al. 2021 (ANOVA on benchmarks), Henderson et al. 2018, Dodge et al. 2020, Sellam et al. 2022, Bui et al. 2025\n- *Prompt/evaluation sensitivity*: Sclar et al. 2024 (76% accuracy swings), Lu et al. 2022, Pezeshkpour & Hruschka 2024, BrittleBench 2026\n- *Infrastructure effects*: Yuan et al. 2025 (9% from hardware/precision), Messina & Scotta 2026 (floating-point non-associativity)\n- *G-theory in LLM eval*: Mesting 2026 (5 facets, single benchmark, judge=43.8%). This paper builds on Mesting.\n- *IRT-based evaluation*: Lalor et al. 2019, Rodriguez et al. 2021, Vania et al. 2021, Polo et al. 2024 (tinyBenchmarks, ~100 items), Perlitz et al. 2023\n- *Alternative frameworks*: Kunievsky & Evans 2025 (intent/articulation/uncertainty), Dehghani et al. 2021 (Benchmark Lottery), Biderman et al. 2024\n\n**References:** 20 references total (page 10-11). Canonical G-theory: Shavelson & Webb 1991, Brennan 2001. Binary G-theory: Shavelson et al. 1989, Mushquash & O'Connor 2006, Jiang & Skorupski 2017.\n\n**Key methodological choices affecting novelty assessment:**\n- Model treated as crossed facet (not fixed) → enables model×item decomposition. This is novel relative to Mesting who treated judge as facet but not model.\n- Judge excluded (exact-match only) → removes largest source from Mesting (43.8%), but limits scope to closed-form metrics.\n- 4 benchmarks × 4 models × full crossing → largest G-theory study in LLM eval to date (~2.3M records).\n- IRT comparison is first direct head-to-head quantification of the item cost gap.\n\n**What the paper does NOT do (from experiment context):**\n- No 14B+ models (exp-005 not completed)\n- No fine-tuning G-study (exp-006 not completed)\n- No generative/open-ended evaluation\n- Hardware study only partial (2/4 models)\n- The originally hypothesized \"gradient\" from knowledge to reasoning benchmarks failed — flat structure reframed as \"benchmark-invariance\"\n\n**Data scale:** 930,400 (MMLU) + 1,382,400 (3 benchmarks) + 230,400 (hardware) = ~2.5M total records.\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nPlease provide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is applying G-theory to LLM evaluation given Mesting (2026) already did it? Are the specific extensions (model-as-facet, cross-benchmark, IRT comparison) sufficiently novel for AAAI? Is this incremental over Mesting or a meaningful advance?\n\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there important missing references? Is the positioning against Mesting (2026) fair? Is the IRT comparison literature adequately covered? Any missing variance decomposition work?\n\n3. **Contribution significance**: Do the three contributions constitute enough novelty for a top venue? Is contribution (3) (benchmark-invariance, reframed from failed gradient hypothesis) convincing as a contribution or does it feel post-hoc?\n\n4. **Framing and narrative**: The title claims \"34%\" but the CI is [28.4, 37.9%] and LOMO shows model selection sensitivity. Is the framing honest? Is \"hidden second factor\" appropriate given Mesting (2026) already exists? The benchmark-invariance was originally supposed to be a gradient — is the reframing transparent or misleading?\n\n5. **Scope limitations and generalizability**: Only 4 models in 7-9B range, only open-weight, only exact-match scoring, only multiple-choice style benchmarks. How much does this limit the claimed contributions?\n\n6. **Writing quality and presentation**: Structure, clarity, figure/table design, honest limitation reporting.\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: [1-10]\n- Related Work Coverage: [1-10]\n- Significance: [1-10]\n- Presentation: [1-10]\n- Overall (R2): [1-10]\n- Confidence: [1-5, where 5=expert]\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":529,"cache_read_input_tokens":60060,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":529},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"891f265c-8c20-445c-964a-94d66c0b1d82","uuid":"c42c4bbc-619b-4a4e-874f-2ad18d8430a5"}
