{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MQw9cL5Ukc7xqEi1w7tfo5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\texp001b Resampling Stability G-Study\n2\t\n3\t| Metric | exp-001 (6f) | Sample 1 | Sample 2 | Sample 3 | Mean±Std | CV% |\n4\t|--------|-------------|----------|----------|----------|----------|-----|\n5\t| item_id% | 61.08 | 75.88 | 75.38 | 72.18 | 74.48±2.01 | 2.7% |\n6\t| prompt×item% | 4.58 | 4.42 | 4.35 | 5.38 | 4.71±0.58 | 12.2% |\n7\t| seed×item% | 4.72 | 5.41 | 4.87 | 5.32 | 5.20±0.29 | 5.5% |\n8\t| temp×item% | 1.83 | 1.43 | 1.44 | 1.66 | 1.51±0.13 | 8.7% |\n9\t| G_item | 0.611 | 0.759 | 0.754 | 0.722 | 0.745±0.020 | 2.7% |\n10\t\n11\tConclusion: item_id dominance stable (CV=2.7%), hypothesis confirmed.\n12\tNote: exp-001b is 5-facet (bf16 only) vs exp-001 6-facet (3 precisions), so absolute % not directly comparable.\n13\tFile: results/analysis/exp001b_resampling_gstudy.json"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HgqizP2Y57DeAR6wSkjoQc","session_id":"891f265c-8c20-445c-964a-94d66c0b1d82","uuid":"bdd2adec-4194-447f-b580-c04448fe770b","timestamp":"2026-05-21T08:53:55.136Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"aa865af373baec683","tool_use_id":"toolu_016Z8EGnZfDErsZbmUcQi9Sf","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1 reads R2's review (Step 2)","usage":{"total_tokens":38373,"tool_uses":7,"duration_ms":64872},"uuid":"e6a48e95-ff69-4670-903f-55b172adf323","session_id":"891f265c-8c20-445c-964a-94d66c0b1d82"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016Z8EGnZfDErsZbmUcQi9Sf","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have sufficient context to write my R1 cross-response. Here is my response:\n\n---\n\n## R1 Cross-Response to R2\n\n### Points of Agreement\n\n**Narrow novelty margin over Mesting.** R2 is right that each extension (model-as-facet, multi-benchmark, IRT comparison) is textbook G-theory. I flagged this in my review as well (the methodological contribution is nil; this is an empirical paper). The paper should own this framing rather than oversell framework novelty.\n\n**\"Hidden Second Factor\" framing is oversold.** I agree that model-by-item interaction is expected in any crossed design — the measurement theory community would not find it surprising. The contribution is the magnitude (33.6%) and practical implication (single-model studies inflate item dominance by ~19pp), not the existence of the interaction. R2's point about the title CI [28.4, 37.9%] vs. the headline \"34%\" is valid; the paper should report the interval prominently.\n\n**Benchmark-invariance as reframed null result.** R2 correctly identifies that Contribution 3 (benchmark-invariance of variance structure) is a post-hoc reframing of the failed gradient hypothesis (R-squared=0.12). I raised the same concern about the n=5 monotonicity claim. The 5.7pp spread being called \"narrow\" when individual CIs span 10-13pp is indeed problematic.\n\n**Related work gaps.** The bibliography at 20 references is thin. I agree that educational measurement cross-test G-studies, GLMM literature, and benchmark contamination are genuine omissions.\n\n**Scope limitations on 4 models and MC-only benchmarks.** My review flagged both the model count concern and the format confound. R2's framing of this as \"severe\" is warranted.\n\n### Points of Disagreement\n\n**R2's Overall=5 underweights the empirical contribution.** While novelty is narrow, the execution quality — 2.5M records, 9 validation analyses, out-of-sample D-study validation (Delta=0.004), the FP32 confound discovery, IRT head-to-head — exceeds the bar for a competent empirical paper. An Overall of 5 implies the paper should be rejected; I think it is borderline-accept territory with revisions. The practitioner toolkit (D-study budgets, benchmark-specific recommendations) has genuine utility that R2 undervalues.\n\n**Sclar reconciliation (R2 Q2) is less damning than implied.** Sclar's 76% swings are prompt-level accuracy swings on individual benchmarks; this paper's 3.8-4.6% is a variance component from a crossed design decomposition. These are different quantities measuring different things. The paper should clarify this distinction, but the gap does not indicate an error.\n\n**Logistic-link G-theory (R2 Q3) is a nice-to-have, not a must-fix.** The logprob scoring robustness check already provides a continuous-metric sensitivity analysis that partially addresses the binary data concern. Implementing Jiang & Skorupski (2017) would strengthen the paper but is not necessary for acceptance if the logprob analysis is elevated.\n\n### New Insights from R2\n\nR2's observation about the Bernoulli variance ceiling creating differential bias across benchmarks (GSM8K p(1-p)=0.250 vs MMLU p(1-p)=0.221) is an important point I did not raise. This means the cross-benchmark gradient may be partially an artifact of accuracy-level differences rather than task complexity differences. This strengthens my concern about format confounds and weakens the gradient claim further.\n\nR2's Q7 (what spread would the authors consider \"wide\"?) is a sharp question that exposes the lack of a pre-specified decision criterion for benchmark invariance.\n\n### Score Adjustments\n\nAfter reading R2, I would adjust:\n\n- **Soundness**: 7 to 6. The Bernoulli ceiling differential across benchmarks is a real methodological concern I underweighted.\n- **Empirical Rigor**: 6 stays. Already accounted for model and scope limitations.\n- **Presentation**: 6 stays.\n- **Overall**: 6 stays. R2's novelty concerns are valid but the execution quality and practitioner value prevent me from going to 5. Borderline-accept with substantial revision remains my position.\n- **Confidence**: 4/5, unchanged."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa865af373baec683 (use SendMessage with to: 'aa865af373baec683' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 39286\ntool_uses: 7\nduration_ms: 64874</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"891f265c-8c20-445c-964a-94d66c0b1d82","uuid":"8a132643-fd06-424f-a29a-f70fe391325b","timestamp":"2026-05-21T08:54:13.883Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** continuing the roundtable discussion. You previously reviewed this paper focusing on **Methods and Experiments**. Now read R2's review (focused on Novelty and Related Work) below, and provide your response.\n\n## Your Previous Scores\n- Soundness: 7, Empirical Rigor: 6, Presentation: 6, Overall: 6, Confidence: 4/5\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty and Related Work)\n\n### Summary\nThis paper applies G-theory to LLM evaluation variance decomposition, extending Mesting (2026) by treating model as a crossed facet, expanding to 4 benchmarks, and comparing G-theory item requirements against IRT. The three claimed contributions are (1) model×item quantification, (2) IRT vs. G-theory item cost comparison, and (3) approximate benchmark-invariance of variance structure. The work is a competent empirical extension of an established psychometric framework to a timely problem, but the novelty margin over Mesting is narrower than the paper's framing suggests, and the third contribution is a reframed null result.\n\n### R2 Strengths\n1. Model-as-crossed-facet is the right design choice and yields a genuinely informative finding (33.6% model×item, single-model inflation 61%→42%).\n2. Out-of-sample D-study validation (Δ=0.004) is methodologically compelling.\n3. IRT vs. G-theory head-to-head fills a real literature gap.\n4. Positioning against Mesting is explicit and largely fair.\n5. LOMO stability analysis provides honest uncertainty quantification.\n6. Scale and robustness battery exceed the standard (~2.5M records, 9 validation analyses).\n\n### R2 Weaknesses\n1. Contribution (3) \"benchmark-invariance\" is a reframed failure of the original gradient hypothesis (R²=0.12). The reframing feels post-hoc. 5.7pp spread is not obviously \"narrow\" given 10-13pp individual CIs.\n2. Novelty margin over Mesting is real but narrow — each extension is textbook G-theory. Framework contribution is nil; purely empirical.\n3. Title claims \"34%\" but CI is [28.4, 37.9%]. \"Hidden Second Factor\" framing questionable — person×item interaction is expected in any crossed G-theory design.\n4. Related work gaps: (a) educational measurement cross-test G-studies, (b) benchmark contamination/data leakage, (c) GLMMs for binary data. Bibliography thin at 20 refs.\n5. \"Hidden Second Factor\" overstates surprise — this is expected from measurement theory, the novelty is magnitude and practical implications.\n6. Scope limitations severe: only 4 models (7-9B), only exact-match, only MC benchmarks.\n\n### R2 Questions\n1. Was benchmark-invariance pre-registered or post-hoc?\n2. How reconcile Sclar 76% swings vs your prompt×item 3.8-4.6%?\n3. Why not implement logistic-link G-theory (Jiang & Skorupski 2017)?\n4. Benchmark contamination impact on item_id?\n5. IRT comparison: are you comparing like with like vs Polo et al.?\n6. D-study 4→10 model projection sensitivity?\n7. What spread would you consider \"wide\" for benchmark invariance?\n\n### R2 Scores\n- Novelty: 5, Related Work: 5, Significance: 6, Presentation: 6, Overall: 5, Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Your Task\n\n1. **Points of agreement**: Where do you agree with R2?\n2. **Points of disagreement**: Where do you disagree, and why?\n3. **New insights**: Did R2's review change your assessment of any aspect?\n4. **Score adjustment**: Would you adjust any of your scores after reading R2? If so, which and why?\n\nKeep your response concise (under 600 words). Focus on substantive differences, not minor points.\n","agentId":"aa865af373baec683","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have sufficient context to write my R1 cross-response. Here is my response:\n\n---\n\n## R1 Cross-Response to R2\n\n### Points of Agreement\n\n**Narrow novelty margin over Mesting.** R2 is right that each extension (model-as-facet, multi-benchmark, IRT comparison) is textbook G-theory. I flagged this in my review as well (the methodological contribution is nil; this is an empirical paper). The paper should own this framing rather than oversell framework novelty.\n\n**\"Hidden Second Factor\" framing is oversold.** I agree that model-by-item interaction is expected in any crossed design — the measurement theory community would not find it surprising. The contribution is the magnitude (33.6%) and practical implication (single-model studies inflate item dominance by ~19pp), not the existence of the interaction. R2's point about the title CI [28.4, 37.9%] vs. the headline \"34%\" is valid; the paper should report the interval prominently.\n\n**Benchmark-invariance as reframed null result.** R2 correctly identifies that Contribution 3 (benchmark-invariance of variance structure) is a post-hoc reframing of the failed gradient hypothesis (R-squared=0.12). I raised the same concern about the n=5 monotonicity claim. The 5.7pp spread being called \"narrow\" when individual CIs span 10-13pp is indeed problematic.\n\n**Related work gaps.** The bibliography at 20 references is thin. I agree that educational measurement cross-test G-studies, GLMM literature, and benchmark contamination are genuine omissions.\n\n**Scope limitations on 4 models and MC-only benchmarks.** My review flagged both the model count concern and the format confound. R2's framing of this as \"severe\" is warranted.\n\n### Points of Disagreement\n\n**R2's Overall=5 underweights the empirical contribution.** While novelty is narrow, the execution quality — 2.5M records, 9 validation analyses, out-of-sample D-study validation (Delta=0.004), the FP32 confound discovery, IRT head-to-head — exceeds the bar for a competent empirical paper. An Overall of 5 implies the paper should be rejected; I think it is borderline-accept territory with revisions. The practitioner toolkit (D-study budgets, benchmark-specific recommendations) has genuine utility that R2 undervalues.\n\n**Sclar reconciliation (R2 Q2) is less damning than implied.** Sclar's 76% swings are prompt-level accuracy swings on individual benchmarks; this paper's 3.8-4.6% is a variance component from a crossed design decomposition. These are different quantities measuring different things. The paper should clarify this distinction, but the gap does not indicate an error.\n\n**Logistic-link G-theory (R2 Q3) is a nice-to-have, not a must-fix.** The logprob scoring robustness check already provides a continuous-metric sensitivity analysis that partially addresses the binary data concern. Implementing Jiang & Skorupski (2017) would strengthen the paper but is not necessary for acceptance if the logprob analysis is elevated.\n\n### New Insights from R2\n\nR2's observation about the Bernoulli variance ceiling creating differential bias across benchmarks (GSM8K p(1-p)=0.250 vs MMLU p(1-p)=0.221) is an important point I did not raise. This means the cross-benchmark gradient may be partially an artifact of accuracy-level differences rather than task complexity differences. This strengthens my concern about format confounds and weakens the gradient claim further.\n\nR2's Q7 (what spread would the authors consider \"wide\"?) is a sharp question that exposes the lack of a pre-specified decision criterion for benchmark invariance.\n\n### Score Adjustments\n\nAfter reading R2, I would adjust:\n\n- **Soundness**: 7 to 6. The Bernoulli ceiling differential across benchmarks is a real methodological concern I underweighted.\n- **Empirical Rigor**: 6 stays. Already accounted for model and scope limitations.\n- **Presentation**: 6 stays.\n- **Overall**: 6 stays. R2's novelty concerns are valid but the execution quality and practitioner value prevent me from going to 5. Borderline-accept with substantial revision remains my position.\n- **Confidence**: 4/5, unchanged."}],"totalDurationMs":64874,"totalTokens":39286,"totalToolUseCount":7,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1552,"cache_read_input_tokens":36685,"output_tokens":1048,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1552},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":1048,"cache_read_input_tokens":36685,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1552,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1552,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":2}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a0a6bbc357be42bb5","tool_use_id":"toolu_01HgqizP2Y57DeAR6wSkjoQc","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2 reads R1's review (Step 2)","usage":{"total_tokens":42245,"tool_uses":13,"duration_ms":62164},"uuid":"49a7154f-63b6-41fa-a7b1-bb16d4ec6edd","session_id":"891f265c-8c20-445c-964a-94d66c0b1d82"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HgqizP2Y57DeAR6wSkjoQc","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the project state, data artifacts, and the discrepancies R1 identified. Let me compose my R2 cross-response.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Cross-Response to R1\n\n### Points of Agreement\n\n**Data artifact inconsistencies are real and concerning.** R1's discovery that `leave_one_model_out.md` reports item_id=51.29% for the full 4-model design while paper_framing.md reports 42.1% is verified. The LOMO matched-spec file confirms 42.07%, meaning the two LOMO artifacts use different analysis specifications (one includes model interactions, one does not). This is not fraud, but it is sloppy bookkeeping that undermines reproducibility claims. The LOMO spread discrepancy (0.023 vs 0.046 vs 0.058 across artifacts) compounds this. I raise my concern level on presentation quality.\n\n**Binary data defense is insufficient.** I flagged this in my initial review but R1 sharpens the point: the Bernoulli correlation of r=0.91 (observed-vs-mechanical variance) versus the paper's framing around r=0.42 (a different quantity -- correlation with item difficulty, not Bernoulli ceiling) is a critical distinction the paper obscures. The 737.5x null simulation proves signal exists but says nothing about bias in the decomposition percentages. I agree this remains the single most damaging methodological gap.\n\n**Exp-002 status is genuinely unclear.** The ROADMAP shows it as \"waiting for submission\" and report.md lists it as \"allocated.\" Yet the round_0 review report discusses cross-benchmark results (5 benchmarks, ~4.25M records) as if they exist. This is a serious integrity question: either the review was conducted on a paper draft containing projected/hypothetical numbers, or the experimental status tracking is unreliable. Either way, R1 is right to flag this as critical.\n\n**Model facet limitations.** 4 models, purposive selection, 3 df -- I agree this is a structural weakness. The bootstrap CIs are wide enough ([28.4, 37.9] for model x item) that the point estimate of 33.6% carries substantial uncertainty.\n\n### Points of Disagreement\n\n**Temperature and ordering as \"random\" facets.** R1 flags these as questionable because they are fixed design points. I partially disagree. G-theory distinguishes between the facet universe and the sampled conditions. Temperature at 3 levels (0.0, 0.3, 0.7) samples from a continuous universe; treating it as random is defensible if the goal is generalizing across temperature settings. Ordering (4 systematic rotations) is weaker -- Latin square rotations are closer to a fixed exhaustive set. But the fixed-vs-random sensitivity analysis showing <5% shift makes this a minor issue in practice.\n\n**Text-exact-match being \"underdeveloped.\"** R1 calls this a weakness; I see it as an appropriately secondary analysis. The finding that temperature dominates at 79.7% under text scoring, making it unreliable at any T>0, is itself a clean result. The paper does not need to develop this into a full parallel analysis -- the metric-conditional contrast is the contribution, not the text metric decomposition per se.\n\n### New Insights from R1\n\nR1's observation about D-study joint optimization being unreproducible is new to me and well-taken. If the budget recommendations in any D-study table cannot be independently computed from the reported variance components, that is a transparency failure for a paper whose practical contribution is precisely those budget tables.\n\nThe numerical inconsistencies R1 catalogued are more extensive than I had appreciated. The fact that multiple artifacts report different numbers for ostensibly the same analysis (LOMO spread at three different values) suggests the analysis pipeline lacks version control discipline -- a serious issue for a reproducibility-focused paper.\n\n### Score Adjustments\n\nAfter reading R1's review:\n\n- **Novelty**: 5 -> 5 (unchanged; R1's findings do not affect novelty assessment)\n- **Related Work**: 5 -> 5 (unchanged)\n- **Significance**: 6 -> 5 (downward; the Exp-002 status ambiguity and numerical inconsistencies reduce confidence in the claimed scope of findings)\n- **Presentation**: 6 -> 5 (downward; artifact inconsistencies and the Bernoulli r=0.91 vs r=0.42 conflation are presentation failures)\n- **Overall**: 5 -> 5 (unchanged; my initial assessment was already cautious)\n- **Confidence**: 4/5 -> 4/5 (unchanged)\n\nThe core issue remains: the paper's strongest contributions (model x item quantification, D-study out-of-sample validation) are genuinely valuable, but they are buried under scope overclaims and numerical sloppiness that a careful revision could fix."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a0a6bbc357be42bb5 (use SendMessage with to: 'a0a6bbc357be42bb5' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 43023\ntool_uses: 13\nduration_ms: 62166</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"891f265c-8c20-445c-964a-94d66c0b1d82","uuid":"562b10ed-6ad1-4fbb-b0d3-907be02dc1e4","timestamp":"2026-05-21T08:54:31.791Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 2 (R2)** continuing the roundtable discussion. You previously reviewed this paper focusing on **Novelty and Related Work**. Now read R1's review (focused on Methods and Experiments) below, and provide your response.\n\n## Your Previous Scores\n- Novelty: 5, Related Work: 5, Significance: 6, Presentation: 6, Overall: 5, Confidence: 4/5\n\n## R1's Review (Methods and Experiments)\n\n### Summary\nThe paper applies G-theory to decompose LLM evaluation variance using Henderson Method I on a balanced 4-model MMLU design (345,600 records). Model×item = 33.6%, second-largest after item (42.1%). Robustness is extensive (9 validations), D-study out-of-sample Δ=0.004 is compelling. However, experimental base is narrower than claims suggest: only Exp-001 (MMLU) fully completed, Exp-002 (cross-benchmark) status unclear, Exp-003 (hardware) partial, Exps 005-008 never executed.\n\n### R1 Strengths\n1. Henderson Method I correctly implemented for balanced design, REML confirmation.\n2. Single-model vs. cross-model comparison is the strongest methodological contribution.\n3. D-study out-of-sample validation is rigorous (truly out-of-sample, 4th model added after 3-model analysis).\n4. Robustness package unusually thorough for NLP (9 validation analyses).\n5. FP32 confound handling is exemplary (vLLM enforce_eager artifact discovery).\n6. Bootstrap CI implementation methodologically appropriate.\n7. IRT vs G-theory comparison fills genuine practical gap.\n\n### R1 Weaknesses\n1. **Critical: Exp-002 (cross-benchmark) appears incomplete but paper claims benchmark-invariance.** Project management shows ALLOCATED status. Cross-benchmark numbers may be unverifiable.\n2. **Critical: Binary data in ANOVA-based G-theory inadequately defended.** Bernoulli variance correlation r=0.91 (not 0.42 as framing suggests) means substantial mechanical confounding. Logistic-link G-theory (Jiang & Skorupski 2017) cited but not implemented.\n3. Model facet: only 4 levels from purposive (non-random) sample. 3 df for variance component. LOMO spread values inconsistent across artifacts (0.058, 0.046, 0.023).\n4. Multiple numerical inconsistencies between data artifacts and reported numbers.\n5. D-study code doesn't implement joint facet optimization — budget recommendations unreproducible.\n6. Hardware study too incomplete for separability claims (2/4 models).\n7. Temperature and ordering as \"random\" facets questionable (fixed design points).\n8. Text-exact-match analysis substantially underdeveloped despite being claimed as contribution.\n\n### R1 Questions\n1. Exp-002 status: What is source of cross-benchmark numbers?\n2. Data artifact inconsistencies: leave_one_model_out reports item_id=51.29% vs paper's 42.1%\n3. LOMO spread: which of 0.058/0.046/0.023 is correct?\n4. Bernoulli confound: r=0.91 vs r=0.42?\n5. D-study joint optimization: how were specific budgets computed?\n6. Seed at T=0: what is the seed×item variance under greedy decoding specifically?\n7. Model universe definition for random facet interpretation?\n\n### R1 Scores\n- Soundness: 7, Empirical Rigor: 6, Presentation: 6, Overall: 6, Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Your Task\n\n1. **Points of agreement**: Where do you agree with R1?\n2. **Points of disagreement**: Where do you disagree, and why?\n3. **New insights**: Did R1's review change your assessment of any aspect?\n4. **Score adjustment**: Would you adjust any of your scores after reading R1? If so, which and why?\n\nKeep your response concise (under 600 words). Focus on substantive differences, not minor points.\n","agentId":"a0a6bbc357be42bb5","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the project state, data artifacts, and the discrepancies R1 identified. Let me compose my R2 cross-response.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Cross-Response to R1\n\n### Points of Agreement\n\n**Data artifact inconsistencies are real and concerning.** R1's discovery that `leave_one_model_out.md` reports item_id=51.29% for the full 4-model design while paper_framing.md reports 42.1% is verified. The LOMO matched-spec file confirms 42.07%, meaning the two LOMO artifacts use different analysis specifications (one includes model interactions, one does not). This is not fraud, but it is sloppy bookkeeping that undermines reproducibility claims. The LOMO spread discrepancy (0.023 vs 0.046 vs 0.058 across artifacts) compounds this. I raise my concern level on presentation quality.\n\n**Binary data defense is insufficient.** I flagged this in my initial review but R1 sharpens the point: the Bernoulli correlation of r=0.91 (observed-vs-mechanical variance) versus the paper's framing around r=0.42 (a different quantity -- correlation with item difficulty, not Bernoulli ceiling) is a critical distinction the paper obscures. The 737.5x null simulation proves signal exists but says nothing about bias in the decomposition percentages. I agree this remains the single most damaging methodological gap.\n\n**Exp-002 status is genuinely unclear.** The ROADMAP shows it as \"waiting for submission\" and report.md lists it as \"allocated.\" Yet the round_0 review report discusses cross-benchmark results (5 benchmarks, ~4.25M records) as if they exist. This is a serious integrity question: either the review was conducted on a paper draft containing projected/hypothetical numbers, or the experimental status tracking is unreliable. Either way, R1 is right to flag this as critical.\n\n**Model facet limitations.** 4 models, purposive selection, 3 df -- I agree this is a structural weakness. The bootstrap CIs are wide enough ([28.4, 37.9] for model x item) that the point estimate of 33.6% carries substantial uncertainty.\n\n### Points of Disagreement\n\n**Temperature and ordering as \"random\" facets.** R1 flags these as questionable because they are fixed design points. I partially disagree. G-theory distinguishes between the facet universe and the sampled conditions. Temperature at 3 levels (0.0, 0.3, 0.7) samples from a continuous universe; treating it as random is defensible if the goal is generalizing across temperature settings. Ordering (4 systematic rotations) is weaker -- Latin square rotations are closer to a fixed exhaustive set. But the fixed-vs-random sensitivity analysis showing <5% shift makes this a minor issue in practice.\n\n**Text-exact-match being \"underdeveloped.\"** R1 calls this a weakness; I see it as an appropriately secondary analysis. The finding that temperature dominates at 79.7% under text scoring, making it unreliable at any T>0, is itself a clean result. The paper does not need to develop this into a full parallel analysis -- the metric-conditional contrast is the contribution, not the text metric decomposition per se.\n\n### New Insights from R1\n\nR1's observation about D-study joint optimization being unreproducible is new to me and well-taken. If the budget recommendations in any D-study table cannot be independently computed from the reported variance components, that is a transparency failure for a paper whose practical contribution is precisely those budget tables.\n\nThe numerical inconsistencies R1 catalogued are more extensive than I had appreciated. The fact that multiple artifacts report different numbers for ostensibly the same analysis (LOMO spread at three different values) suggests the analysis pipeline lacks version control discipline -- a serious issue for a reproducibility-focused paper.\n\n### Score Adjustments\n\nAfter reading R1's review:\n\n- **Novelty**: 5 -> 5 (unchanged; R1's findings do not affect novelty assessment)\n- **Related Work**: 5 -> 5 (unchanged)\n- **Significance**: 6 -> 5 (downward; the Exp-002 status ambiguity and numerical inconsistencies reduce confidence in the claimed scope of findings)\n- **Presentation**: 6 -> 5 (downward; artifact inconsistencies and the Bernoulli r=0.91 vs r=0.42 conflation are presentation failures)\n- **Overall**: 5 -> 5 (unchanged; my initial assessment was already cautious)\n- **Confidence**: 4/5 -> 4/5 (unchanged)\n\nThe core issue remains: the paper's strongest contributions (model x item quantification, D-study out-of-sample validation) are genuinely valuable, but they are buried under scope overclaims and numerical sloppiness that a careful revision could fix."}],"totalDurationMs":62166,"totalTokens":43023,"totalToolUseCount":13,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7189,"cache_read_input_tokens":34693,"output_tokens":1140,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7189},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":1140,"cache_read_input_tokens":34693,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7189,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7189,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":7}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ki9q8hqpPTjedsT43T7JUy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the cross-sharing results. Let me proceed to Step 3 (convergence) and Step 4 (synthesis).\n\nLet me summarize the state:\n\n**R1 after cross-sharing:**\n- Soundness: 7→6, Empirical Rigor: 6, Presentation: 6, Overall: 6\n- Key agreements: narrow novelty, oversold \"Hidden\" framing, benchmark-invariance is post-hoc, scope limitations severe\n- Key disagreement: R2's Overall=5 underweights execution quality; Sclar reconciliation is less damaging; logistic-link is nice-to-have\n  \n**R2 after cross-sharing:**\n- Significance: 6→5, Presentation: 6→5, Overall: 5\n- Key agreements: data inconsistencies real, binary data defense insufficient, Exp-002 status unclear\n- Key disagreement: temperature as random facet is defensible; text-exact-match is appropriately secondary\n\nNow I'm identifying the core tensions for convergence: the overall score gap (R1=6 vs R2=5), whether strong execution can offset limited novelty, and the severity of the remaining technical issues.\n\nBoth reviewers converge on the genuine value of the model×item interaction finding and the compelling D-study validation, while agreeing that the binary data handling is the most significant methodological weakness, the benchmark-invariance claim is overstated, and the Exp-002 ambiguity raises serious concerns. I'll now synthesize these points into a final assessment rather than continuing the back-and-forth. I'm clarifying the AAAI scale interpretation—a 5 represents borderline with genuine merit but significant concerns, while a 6 would indicate weak acceptance. Given the paper's solid empirical contribution offset by narrow novelty, weak third contribution, and methodological issues with binary data and limited model scope, I'm settling on a 5 with a lean toward rejection, though the core findings are sound enough that targeted revisions could push it higher. Now I'll draft the complete review.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4769,"cache_read_input_tokens":73917,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4769},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"891f265c-8c20-445c-964a-94d66c0b1d82","uuid":"e2618f39-a915-4d47-93da-138abe2df1f8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ki9q8hqpPTjedsT43T7JUy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Step 2 交叉分享完成。双方已识别共识与分歧。现在进入 Step 3（收敛）和 Step 4（综合报告撰写与提交）。\n\n**收敛要点整理：**\n\nR1（调整后）：Soundness 6, Empirical Rigor 6, Presentation 6, Overall **6**\nR2（调整后）：Novelty 5, Significance 5, Presentation 5, Overall **5**\n\n**共识：**\n- model×item = 33.6% + D-study Δ=0.004 是真实有价值的发现\n- Binary data 在 ANOVA G-theory 中的理论防御不足（Bernoulli r=0.91）\n- Contribution 3（benchmark-invariance）是 post-hoc reframe，说服力弱\n- 标题 \"34%\" 过度确定（CI [28.4, 37.9%]）；\"Hidden\" 夸大惊喜度\n- 4 模型、7-9B、MC-only 严重限制泛化性\n- 数据 artifacts 数值不一致令人担忧\n- 相关工作有缺口（cross-test G-studies, benchmark contamination, GLMM）\n\n**分歧：** R1 认为执行质量值 6 分（weak accept）；R2 认为 novelty 天花板低，5 分（borderline）。\n\n现在撰写综合审稿报告并提交。"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4769,"cache_read_input_tokens":73917,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4769},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"891f265c-8c20-445c-964a-94d66c0b1d82","uuid":"3e0ddade-652b-4a18-8544-53b0526d9aee"}
